The matching's own marks go straight to the panel, and it says how far it got

The boot of run 543 stopped inside IOService::probeCandidates, and the
last thing it printed was the result of one candidate's probe.  The
kernel was alive -- the beacon kept writing its letters -- so the
matching had not stopped; it was spending its time in the console.

Every candidate printed four lines through printf, and printf goes
through the console's ring buffer: it takes the console lock, copies the
text into the ring, and wakes whoever empties it.  The ring is emptied
one character at a time through the console blob's own writer, so a
candidate's lines cost a lock, a wakeup and a few hundred single
character writes, and the matching does that for every candidate it
tries.

There is a way to write to the panel that does none of that.  The
console blob's writer takes a character at a time and puts it straight
into the framebuffer, with no lock and no ring, and this tree already
uses it for the marks that have to appear even when the console path is
the thing that is stuck:

  /* nio: put a short mark on the framebuffer through the console
   * blob's own writer -- no lock, no ring. */
  static void
  nio_u_mark(const char * s)
  {
      while (*s != '\0') {
          cons_ops[1].putc(*s++, true);
      }
  }

So the six prints that fire per candidate now go through that writer
instead of through printf.  They say the same things and they still
appear in the same place on the panel, but they do not take the console
lock and they do not go into the ring.  The print that says the
candidate loop has finished goes the same way, so that the end of the
matching is visible even when the console is not.

And the loop now leaves a mark of its own: a Q with the candidate count
in hex, every sixty-four times the loop is entered.  The Q that was
already there says that the loop was entered; this one says how many
times, so a panel where the last Q counts up says the matching is
moving, and a panel where the last Q is the same one as the one before
says it stopped there.

The prints that fire once per nub -- the ones that say a nub matched,
that the configuration thread is alive, that a match was dropped -- are
left where they are: there are only a few of them and they are the ones
worth reading.

